        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 做客",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "做客 — zuòkè — \"to visit as a guest; to be a guest\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "我去他家做客喝了两杯茶 wǒ qù tā jiā zuòkè hē le liǎng bēi chá — I went round to his place and had two cups of tea."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "做客 (zuòkè) is built from 做 'to do, to be' and 客 'guest'. The second is inherited from Middle Chinese khaek (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), glossed 'guest'; 做 has no form in that dictionary, so the history of the first part is not settled."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for talking about plans."
